<header><p class="st-kicker">republic · South America</p><h1 class="st-title">Republic of New Granada</h1><p class="st-meta">1831 CE – 1858 CE · capital Bogotá</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The centralist republic formed from Gran Colombia's core around Bogotá, during which Colombia's Liberal and Conservative parties were born.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>After <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/venezuela">Venezuela</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/republic-of-ecuador">Ecuador</a> left <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/gran-colombia">Gran Colombia</a> in 1830, the remaining provinces, including <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/republic-of-panama">Panama</a>, formed the Republic of New Granada under a constitution of 1832. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francisco-de-paula-santander">Francisco de Paula Santander</a> returned from exile and served as president from 1832 to 1837.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The republic faced regional revolts, notably the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-of-the-supremes">War of the Supremes</a> from 1839 to 1842. Colombia's Liberal and Conservative parties emerged in 1848 and 1849. The liberal president <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jose-hilario-lopez">José Hilario López</a> carried out major reforms, abolishing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a> in 1851 and expelling the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesuits">Jesuits</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/panama-railroad">Panama Railway</a>, completed in 1855 during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/california">California</a> gold rush, made the isthmus a world trade route. The constitution of 1853 introduced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/universal-suffrage">universal male suffrage</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Pressure for federalism grew, and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/republic-of-panama">Panama</a> became a self-governing state in 1855. The constitution of 1858 turned the republic into the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/granadine-confederation">Granadine Confederation</a>. Its Liberal and Conservative parties dominated Colombian politics into the 21st century.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/republic-of-new-granada.jpg" alt="Republic of New Granada" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: Fibonacci · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_New_Granada.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1831 CE – 1858 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>republic</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Bogotá</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Francisco de Paula Santander, Tomás Cipriano de Mosquera, José Hilario López</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Roman Catholicism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Spanish</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/colombia">Colombia</a>, <a href="/countries/panama">Panama</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>República de la Nueva Granada</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1844&amp;focus=republic-of-new-granada" aria-label="Republic of New Granada on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="2625 2005 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Republic of New Granada lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M2945 2261L2945 2261M2945 2261L2945 2261M2945 2261L2804 2099"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M2945 2261h0M2945 2261h0M2804 2099h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M2945 2261h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M2945 2261h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Bogotá. <a href="/atlas?year=1844&amp;focus=republic-of-new-granada">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
